Piezoelectricity, symmetry and mechanical constraints (@mat-piezoelectric: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM", #materials-engineering "Pyroelectric-and-thermoelectric-materials.lingua#r_0GEX1Y9047ZM1RRK5NZMQW4HG9") { r_5ZGBZAXYXP5F3QA2XMPWVF16HF
**Read:** [Functional material response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM), the whole Piezoelectricity package, including poling and depolarization; revisit the shared tensor and mechanical-boundary foundations.

In a consistent small-signal convention, strain S=sᴱT+dᵀE and electric displacement D=dT+εᵀE. Here T denotes stress, not temperature; superscripts identify quantities measured at fixed field or stress. Mechanical clamping changes the effective electrical response. The converse and direct effects belong to the same coupled energy account.

Crystal symmetry constrains which tensor components can exist. A random unpoled ceramic aggregate can average out responses that individual domains possess. Poling changes domain populations; temperature, field and stress history can undo or modify the useful state.

**Work:** Derive the one-dimensional open-circuit versus short-circuit response and check units of d in C/N and m/V. Compare a force sensor and displacement actuator through geometry, preload, resonance, losses and thermal limits. Distinguish a reversible small-signal coefficient from a large switching strain.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
